---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import MctsExplainer from '../../../nn-chess/ui/MctsExplainer.tsx';

// The body is a tagged template string because Astro parses `{...}` in
// markup as JSX expressions; nearly every LaTeX fragment here contains
// braces (e.g. `\sum_{a'}`), and writing them inline would force every
// brace to be escaped as `&#123;`. Keeping the content in a single
// `set:html` payload avoids that and keeps the math human-readable.
//
// Watch out for `${...}` literal sequences anywhere in these template
// strings — that *would* be interpolated. None of the LaTeX here puts
// `{` immediately after `$`, so we're safe; if you add new math be
// careful to keep at least one character between `$` and `{`.
const intro = String.raw`
  <h1>How nn-chess thinks: PUCT-style MCTS, sims/move, and cPuct</h1>
  <p><a href="/nn-chess">← back to play</a></p>

  <p>
    Every time the nn-chess opponent picks a move, it runs a small
    <strong><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monte_Carlo_tree_search">Monte
    Carlo Tree Search</a></strong> (MCTS) rooted at the current position.
    The specific variant is called <strong>PUCT</strong> — "Predictor +
    UCT," a bandit-style action-selection rule we'll define carefully in
    §2. The two knobs the main page exposes,
    <em>sims/move</em> and <em>cPuct</em>, are both parameters of that
    rule. This page is a math-first walk-through of what the search
    does, why it does it that way, and what those two knobs actually
    control; the end of the page is an interactive mate-in-2 that uses
    your own trained networks to drive each MCTS call.
  </p>

  <h2>1. The shape of the search</h2>

  <p>
    Classical full-width
    <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minimax">minimax</a> explores
    every legal move to a fixed depth and is hopeless for chess: with a
    branching factor around 35 and a value network costing real
    wall-clock time per evaluation, even depth-6 minimax dwarfs the
    per-move budget we have in the browser. MCTS gives up exhaustive
    coverage in exchange for a budget we can dial: it spends $n$
    simulations exploring the tree, and the tree it builds is highly
    asymmetric — deep along promising lines, shallow elsewhere.
  </p>

  <p>
    Concretely, MCTS treats the game as a tree. A <strong>node</strong>
    is a board position. An <strong>edge</strong> is a legal move
    leading from one node to another; we'll usually write an edge as a
    pair $(s, a)$ — "the action $a$ available at state $s$." The
    <strong>root</strong> is the position we're choosing a move for. A
    <strong>leaf</strong> is a node we haven't yet expanded — either
    because it's terminal (game over) or because the search hasn't
    spent a simulation on it yet. One "simulation" or "playout" is a
    single walk from root to leaf plus an evaluation and a bookkeeping
    pass back up the path.
  </p>

  <p>
    The flavour of MCTS we use is the <strong>PUCT</strong> variant from
    <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AlphaGo_Zero">AlphaGo
    Zero</a><sup id="fnref-1"><a href="#fn-1">[1]</a></sup> and
    <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AlphaZero">AlphaZero</a><sup
    id="fnref-2"><a href="#fn-2">[2]</a></sup>. A
    <em>single</em> neural network ("the net" — one set of weights,
    two output heads) supplies:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>a <em>prior policy</em> $P(s, a)$ — a probability distribution
        over legal moves from $s$, produced by the policy head and
        normalised with <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Softmax_function">softmax</a>;</li>
    <li>a <em>value</em> $V(s) \in [-1, 1]$ — the value head's estimate
        of how good $s$ is for whichever side is to move at $s$.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The only terminal-reward signal the search ever sees is fixed:
    $+1$ if the side that just moved delivered checkmate, $-1$ if it
    got checkmated, $0$ for any draw. <em>That is the only chess
    heuristic baked in.</em> Everything else — piece values, tactics,
    positional understanding — has to come out of the network from
    games.
  </p>

  <p>One simulation has four phases, run repeatedly for the search budget:</p>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>Selection.</strong> Starting at the root, descend through
        the tree by repeatedly picking the edge with the highest PUCT
        score (defined in §2) until you reach a node that's either
        unexpanded (we've never asked the net about it) or terminal.</li>
    <li><strong>Expansion.</strong> If the leaf is non-terminal, ask
        the net for $P(\cdot \mid s)$ and $V(s)$. Mask $P$ to the legal
        moves at $s$ and renormalise. Create one child edge per legal
        move and store its prior.</li>
    <li><strong>Evaluation.</strong> Use $V(s)$ as the value to back up.
        If the leaf is terminal, use the terminal reward instead.
        Classical MCTS does an actual <em>rollout</em><sup
        id="fnref-3"><a href="#fn-3">[3]</a></sup> here — it plays a
        random or weakly-policy-driven game from the leaf to the end,
        and backs up the win/loss. AlphaZero showed that once you have
        a competent value head, the value head replaces what the rollout
        used to estimate; no random play is needed at all.</li>
    <li><strong>Backup.</strong> Walk back up the path you descended,
        incrementing visit counts and accumulating value along each
        edge. Because chess is
        <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zero-sum_game">zero-sum</a>
        and turn-alternating, the value flips sign at every step — a
        position worth $+0.4$ to me is worth $-0.4$ to my opponent, who
        is to move one ply earlier. §3 makes this precise.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    After all simulations finish, the move chosen at the root is the
    one with the most visits. The visit-count distribution also doubles
    as a <em>policy target</em> when the net is being trained: during
    AlphaZero-style self-play the policy head is regressed toward
    $\pi(a) \propto N(s, a)$, on the theory that the search produces an
    improvement over the raw prior. We're not training on this page —
    everything below is inference — but the same machinery feeds the
    training step on the main page.
  </p>

  <h2>2. The PUCT formula in detail</h2>

  <p>
    Each edge $(s, a)$ in the search tree carries four numbers we
    update as the search runs:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>$P(s, a)$ — the prior from the net's policy head, fixed at
        expansion time and not touched afterwards.</li>
    <li>$N(s, a)$ — visit count: the number of simulations that have
        descended through this edge so far.</li>
    <li>$W(s, a)$ — total backed-up value through this edge (the sum
        of the values each of those $N(s, a)$ simulations contributed
        on their way up).</li>
    <li>$Q(s, a) = W(s, a) / N(s, a)$ — the running average value
        of this edge. Defined to be $0$ for edges that have never been
        visited.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Selection at $s$ picks the legal action $a$ that maximises:</p>

  $$
    \mathrm{PUCT}(s, a) \;=\; \underbrace{Q(s, a)}_{\text{exploit}}
    \;+\; \underbrace{c_{\text{puct}} \cdot P(s, a) \cdot \dfrac{\sqrt{N(s)}}{1 + N(s, a)}}_{\text{explore } U(s,a)}
  $$

  <p>
    where $N(s) = \sum_{a'} N(s, a')$ is the total visit count at the
    parent node $s$ (the sum of $N$ over all the edges leaving it).
    Both terms in the PUCT sum are interpretable:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Exploit.</strong> $Q(s, a)$ is the running average outcome
      from this edge so far, in $[-1, 1]$ from the perspective of the
      side to move at $s$. If the network keeps producing
      side-to-move-wins signals through some edge, its $Q$ stays
      positive and the search gravitates there.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Explore.</strong> $U(s, a)$ is large when (a) the prior
      is high — the policy head likes this move — and (b) we haven't
      visited it much yet. As $N(s, a) \to \infty$ for one edge, its
      $U$ decays as $1 / N(s, a)$ while $Q$ stays bounded; the
      exploration bonus eventually vanishes and selection is dominated
      by exploitation.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Why $\sqrt{N(s)}$ in the explore bonus? It's a heuristic choice
    from the AlphaGo Zero paper, loosely inspired by
    <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-armed_bandit#Upper_confidence_bound_(UCB)_algorithms">UCB1</a><sup
    id="fnref-4"><a href="#fn-4">[4]</a></sup> — the canonical
    exploration bonus for stochastic
    <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-armed_bandit">multi-armed
    bandit</a> problems. UCB1's bonus is $\sqrt{\log N(s) / N(s, a)}$,
    derived from a Chernoff-style confidence bound on the empirical
    mean of an arm; PUCT swaps the $\log$ for a plain $\sqrt{N(s)}$ and
    multiplies the whole thing by the policy prior $P(s, a)$ so
    high-prior moves get the bulk of early exploration. The net effect:
    visits distribute roughly in proportion to
    $\sqrt{N(s)} \cdot P(s, a)$ early on (when $N(s, a)$ is small and
    $Q$ is noisy), then concentrate on whichever edges develop a high
    $Q$.
  </p>

  <p>
    All ties on $Q + U$ are broken deterministically (high prior first,
    then lexicographic UCI). Search is otherwise purely deterministic
    once the network weights and rng seed are fixed — see the
    determinism test in <code>core/mcts/mcts.test.ts</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>3. The backup, with sign-flipping</h2>

  <p>
    During backup, the value $v$ produced at the leaf travels back up
    the descent path. Because chess is zero-sum and turn-alternating,
    adjacent plies belong to opposite players: if the leaf is worth
    $+0.7$ to the side to move <em>there</em>, the same outcome is
    worth $-0.7$ to its parent's mover, $+0.7$ to the grandparent's
    mover, and so on. So at each step up the path we negate $v$ before
    crediting it to the edge:
  </p>

  $$
    v \;\leftarrow\; -v, \quad
    N(s, a) \mathrel{+}= 1, \quad
    W(s, a) \mathrel{+}= v
  $$

  <p>
    This convention keeps every $Q(s, a)$ in the tree measured from the
    side-to-move's perspective at $s$, which is exactly what selection
    needs to compare candidate moves consistently.
  </p>

  <p>
    Terminal leaves get the same flip in disguise. If the leaf is a
    checkmate, the side that just moved won — so $+1$ for them, $-1$
    for the side to move <em>at the leaf</em> (the side that's mated).
    Our backup convention measures everything from the side-to-move's
    perspective, so the value we want to push up is the second of those
    two — $-1$ at the leaf. The helper <code>terminalValueForMover</code>
    in <code>core/mcts/index.ts</code> answers from the just-moved
    side's perspective (because that's the convenient one for the rules
    module), so the search code negates it once before sending it up
    the path.
  </p>

  <h2>4. What sims/move actually controls</h2>

  <p>
    The <em>sims/move</em> slider on the main page caps the number of
    PUCT simulations the search runs before picking a move. A few
    quantitative things follow from increasing it:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Visit distribution sharpens.</strong> In the limit
      $n \to \infty$, MCTS's visit-count distribution
      $\pi_n(a) = N(s, a) / n$ concentrates on the action that maximises
      the average backed-up $Q$ — the best move <em>according to the
      value head as currently trained</em>, not the move that is best
      in some game-theoretic ground-truth sense. (At training time,
      AlphaZero sampled training moves with a temperature,
      $\pi_T(a) \propto N(s, a)^{1/T}$, to keep the data generator
      exploring; we play greedy at inference, so $T = 0$ and we just
      take the argmax visit count.<sup id="fnref-5"><a
      href="#fn-5">[5]</a></sup>)
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Forced mates become discoverable.</strong> A mate-in-$k$
      from the side to move requires the search to reach a terminal
      mating leaf along at least one defensive subtree at every
      intermediate ply. With branching factor $b$ and uniform (cold-
      start) priors, PUCT spreads visits roughly $b$-ways at each ply,
      so reaching one specific terminal leaf $k$ plies down takes on
      the order of $b^k$ simulations before the $+1$ first propagates
      back to the root.<sup id="fnref-6"><a href="#fn-6">[6]</a></sup>
      In practice the search is much more efficient than this bound
      because PUCT clusters visits on a small subset of moves at each
      ply, and $Q$ shapes that clustering as soon as the first terminal
      leaves are touched; but the exponential is why doubling sims/move
      has such an outsized effect on tactical strength.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cost is linear in sims.</strong> Each simulation costs
      one forward pass of the network (in the worst case — repeated
      states within a single search hit an in-memory cache keyed on
      <code>repetitionKey</code>) plus a few arithmetic ops per node on
      the descent path. The descent path's length is bounded by however
      deep PUCT decides to go, which in our cold-start regime rarely
      passes a few plies before hitting an unexpanded leaf, so the
      per-simulation cost is dominated by the forward pass. Doubling
      sims/move therefore doubles wall-clock per move.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Diminishing returns once the search is informed.</strong>
      Once the policy net has $Q$ that already orders moves well, extra
      sims mostly reinforce the same top edge. The interesting regime
      for nn-chess is the cold-start one, where doubling sims/move
      actually lets the terminal $+1$ from a mating line reach the root
      that it couldn't reach before.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>5. What cPuct actually controls</h2>

  <p>
    $c_{\text{puct}}$ is a single scalar that determines how much weight
    selection puts on $U$ relative to $Q$. The two boundary cases are
    instructive:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>$c_{\text{puct}} \to 0$:</strong> the explore term
      vanishes and selection becomes "pick the edge with the highest
      $Q$, breaking ties on $N$, then on prior, then lexicographically."
      With $Q$ initialised to $0$ everywhere, every edge ties at the
      root on the first pass; the prior-based tiebreak picks the
      highest-prior move first. Once that one edge has been visited,
      its child's $V$ flows up and gives it a nonzero $Q$, which then
      beats every other edge's $Q = 0$ forever — so the search locks
      onto a single line and never reconsiders. Useful only after the
      search has already converged via a nonzero $c_{\text{puct}}$.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>$c_{\text{puct}} \to \infty$:</strong> $Q$ becomes
      negligible and selection picks
      $a = \mathrm{argmax}_a P(s, a) / (1 + N(s, a))$. This sweeps
      through moves roughly in prior order without ever exploiting the
      backed-up values. Search behaves like pure rollout-budget
      apportionment by the prior and never converges on the best move.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    AlphaZero used $c_{\text{puct}} = 1.5$ for chess; that's the
    nn-chess default. The right value depends on (a) the value-head
    scale (ours is $\tanh$, so $Q \in [-1, 1]$) and (b) how peaked the
    prior is. A near-uniform prior — what you get from a cold-start
    net — calls for a smaller $c_{\text{puct}}$, because $U$ is then
    much flatter and adding more of it just spreads visits across edges
    $Q$ has already ranked. A very peaked prior calls for a larger
    $c_{\text{puct}}$ to overcome the policy's confidence when it's
    wrong.
  </p>

  <p>
    Concretely on the demo below: with the cold-start nets nn-chess
    ships with, a value of $c_{\text{puct}}$ around $1$–$1.5$ is enough
    that the terminal signal from <code>Qd7+ Kf8 Qf7#</code> can
    propagate back through MCTS. Push it up to $4$ and you'll see
    visits scatter across many root moves and the mate becomes harder
    to land at low sim counts. Push it down to $0.25$ and you'll see
    the search lock onto whichever queen move happened to get the first
    above-zero $Q$.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Dirichlet noise (briefly)</h2>

  <p>
    The MCTS module supports root-only
    <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dirichlet_distribution">Dirichlet</a>
    noise:
    $\tilde P(s_0, a) = (1 - \varepsilon) P(s_0, a) + \varepsilon \eta_a$
    where $\eta \sim \mathrm{Dir}(\alpha)$. This is how AlphaZero forces
    a bit of exploration during self-play (it keeps the data generator
    from collapsing onto a single line). We use it in self-play games
    against the imitator with the standard chess values
    $\alpha = 0.3$, $\varepsilon = 0.25$<sup id="fnref-7"><a
    href="#fn-7">[7]</a></sup>; we do <em>not</em> use it during play
    against the user — at inference time we want the best move, not an
    exploration-noised one. See <code>SELFPLAY_SEARCH</code> in
    <code>nn-chess/ui/opponent-config.ts</code> for where this is wired
    in. The demo below also leaves Dirichlet noise off so the visit
    counts you see are pure PUCT.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Interactive demo: a mate-in-2 against your own nets</h2>

  <p>
    The walkthrough below loads your stored opponent and imitator
    networks from this browser's IndexedDB (the same ones the main page
    plays against and trains). At each position it runs MCTS with the
    sims-per-move and cPuct you set, and lists the top candidate moves
    with their $N$, $Q$, $P$, and the
    $U = c_{\text{puct}} \cdot P \cdot \sqrt{N(s)} / (1 + N(s, a))$
    that the <em>next</em> simulation would add. Click any candidate
    row to play that move and see MCTS think about the resulting
    position; the demo goes up to four half-moves deep so you can walk
    the full forced sequence and one extra ply of slack for offline
    exploration.
  </p>

  <p>
    White (the winning side) uses the <code>opponent</code> network;
    black (the defender) uses the <code>imitator</code>. Both have to
    have been initialised — if you've never visited the main page, click
    over there once first so the random-init weights get persisted.
  </p>

  <p>A few things worth poking at:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Crank sims/move up.</strong> At very low sims the search
      may not reach the mate; the visit count on <code>d1d7</code>
      (Qd7+) may be no larger than other queen moves. Increase sims/move
      and watch the visit count concentrate.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Play a non-mating move and look at the resulting $Q$.</strong>
      The new position should produce $Q$ values close to $0$ across
      candidates — the opponent's untrained value head doesn't know
      either side is materially ahead — but the leftover decisive lines
      still bias visits.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sweep cPuct.</strong> At $c_{\text{puct}} = 0.25$ you'll
      see the search lock onto one edge; at $c_{\text{puct}} = 4$ you'll
      see visits spread across many moves with $Q$ values barely
      moving from the prior order.
    </li>
  </ul>
`;

const outro = String.raw`
  <h2>8. Where this lives in the code</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>nn-chess/core/mcts/index.ts</code> — the entire search.
      <code>SearchResult</code> exposes <code>visitCounts</code>,
      <code>priorPolicy</code>, and <code>edgeValues</code> ($Q$ for
      each root edge); the table above is rendered from those.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>nn-chess/core/opponent/index.ts</code> —
      <code>policyFromNet</code> adapts a <code>ChessNet</code> to the
      <code>Policy</code> interface MCTS consumes; <code>selectMove</code>
      runs the search and applies the temperature sampler.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>nn-chess/ui/opponent-config.ts</code> — where the production
      defaults for sims/move, cPuct, and Dirichlet noise live.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Footnotes &amp; further reading</h2>

  <ol class="footnotes" style="font-size: 0.92em; color: #333;">
    <li id="fn-1" style="margin-bottom: 8px;">
      Silver et al., <em>Mastering the game of Go without human
      knowledge</em>, Nature 550, 354–359 (2017).
      <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nature24270">Nature</a>;
      the paper that introduced AlphaGo Zero and the PUCT formula
      we use here.
      <a href="#fnref-1" aria-label="Return to text">↩</a>
    </li>
    <li id="fn-2" style="margin-bottom: 8px;">
      Silver et al., <em>A general reinforcement learning algorithm
      that masters chess, shogi, and Go through self-play</em>, Science
      362, 1140–1144 (2018);
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.01815">arXiv:1712.01815</a>.
      The AlphaZero paper, which carries the same PUCT machinery over
      to chess (and is where the $c_{\text{puct}} = 1.5$ default comes
      from).
      <a href="#fnref-2" aria-label="Return to text">↩</a>
    </li>
    <li id="fn-3" style="margin-bottom: 8px;">
      The original MCTS line of work — Coulom (2006), Kocsis &amp;
      Szepesvári (2006), and the broader UCT family — relies on these
      random rollouts and works without a learned value head at all.
      See Browne et al., <em>A Survey of Monte Carlo Tree Search
      Methods</em>, IEEE Transactions on Computational Intelligence and
      AI in Games (2012),
      <a href="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/6145622">DOI:
      10.1109/TCIAIG.2012.2186810</a>, for the pre-AlphaGo state of the
      art. The Wikipedia page on
      <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monte_Carlo_tree_search#Principle_of_operation">MCTS</a>
      walks through the rollout-based version step by step.
      <a href="#fnref-3" aria-label="Return to text">↩</a>
    </li>
    <li id="fn-4" style="margin-bottom: 8px;">
      Auer, Cesa-Bianchi &amp; Fischer, <em>Finite-time Analysis of the
      Multiarmed Bandit Problem</em>, Machine Learning 47, 235–256
      (2002),
      <a href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1023/A:1013689704352">Springer</a>.
      The paper that derives UCB1. For PUCT specifically, the relevant
      precursor is Rosin's <em>Multi-armed bandits with episode
      context</em> (2011),
      <a href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10472-011-9258-6">Springer</a>,
      which introduces the prior-weighted variant the AlphaGo Zero
      paper builds on.
      <a href="#fnref-4" aria-label="Return to text">↩</a>
    </li>
    <li id="fn-5" style="margin-bottom: 8px;">
      AlphaZero used $T = 1$ for the first $30$ plies of each self-play
      game (so the data generator could see varied openings) and then
      $T \to 0$ for the rest of the game. The
      <code>SELFPLAY_TEMPERATURE_PLIES</code> constant in
      <code>nn-chess/ui/opponent-config.ts</code> follows the same
      convention. <code>nn-chess</code>'s self-play uses $T = 1$ during
      that opening window and greedy after; the human-play opponent is
      always greedy.
      <a href="#fnref-5" aria-label="Return to text">↩</a>
    </li>
    <li id="fn-6" style="margin-bottom: 8px;">
      This is a very loose worst-case bound. With a uniform prior and
      no value-head signal, a fresh search has nothing to push visits
      one way or the other, so all $b^k$ leaves $k$ plies down are
      roughly equally likely to be reached first. As soon as a few
      terminal leaves <em>are</em> reached, the resulting $+1$/$-1$
      values flow up through $Q$ and bias selection sharply, which is
      why the empirical sims-to-mate is much smaller than $b^k$ in
      practice.
      <a href="#fnref-6" aria-label="Return to text">↩</a>
    </li>
    <li id="fn-7" style="margin-bottom: 8px;">
      Dirichlet noise is applied <em>only</em> at the root and never
      inside the tree. Adding it deeper would corrupt the recursive
      structure of $Q$ — child evaluations would no longer be reusable
      for selection at the same state in a future simulation, because
      the priors that determined their visit counts would have been
      different. Restricting noise to the root keeps the per-state
      tree consistent while still letting the data generator wander
      across distinct opening choices.
      <a href="#fnref-7" aria-label="Return to text">↩</a>
    </li>
  </ol>
`;
